% What a subinterval keeps alive, run through `xpost -d null` by
% run-ps-test.sh: prints SUCCESS iff every assertion holds.
%
% How this is measured. The used figure vmstatus reports does not fall when
% memory is reclaimed, so comparing it before and after a collection says
% nothing -- a run that reclaims everything and a run that reclaims nothing
% report the same number. Reclamation shows as reuse instead: drop the
% object, collect, allocate the same shape again, and see whether the second
% allocation costs anything. Nothing means the first was recovered and handed
% back; full price means it was not.

% COLLECTION: this reads REUSE rather than a difference -- the header
% below says why -- so a collection cannot make a reading mean
% something else. It is NOT insensitive to collection frequency: below
% the shipped count the freed block is not reused at any request size,
% and the cause is not settled. It passes at the shipped count and
% above. Turning collection off was tried and did not change it.

/failcount 0 def
/assert { exch { pop } { (FAIL: ) print print (\n) print /failcount failcount 1 add store } ifelse } def
/used { vmstatus pop exch pop } bind def

% --- a composite nothing refers to is reclaimed and reused ----------------

/a 60000 array def
/a null def
1 vmreclaim
/mark1 used def
/b 60000 array def
1 vmreclaim
used mark1 sub /arraycost exch def
/b null def
1 vmreclaim
arraycost 1000 lt (an unreferenced array is reclaimed and its space reused) assert

/s 60000 string def
/s null def
1 vmreclaim
/mark2 used def
/t 60000 string def
1 vmreclaim
used mark2 sub /stringcost exch def
/t null def
1 vmreclaim
stringcost 1000 lt (an unreferenced string is reclaimed and its space reused) assert

% --- a subinterval keeps its own contents readable ------------------------
% Whatever the collector does with the rest, the window itself must survive
% and still read the elements it was cut from.

/host 600 array def
0 1 599 { dup host 3 1 roll put } for
/win host 100 42 getinterval def
win length 42 eq (a subinterval reports its own length) assert
win 0 get 100 eq (a subinterval reads the element it starts at) assert
win 41 get 141 eq (a subinterval reads its last element) assert
/host null def
1 vmreclaim
win 0 get 100 eq (a subinterval still reads after its host is dropped) assert
win 41 get 141 eq (a subinterval still reads its last element after that) assert

/shost 600 string def
/swin shost 100 42 getinterval def
swin length 42 eq (a string subinterval reports its own length) assert
/shost null def
1 vmreclaim
swin length 42 eq (a string subinterval survives its host being dropped) assert

% -------------------------------------------------------------------------
% KNOWN DEVIATION (TODO, not asserted): a subinterval holds its whole backing
% composite alive, not just the elements it spans.
% Measured by the reuse method above: with a 42-element window kept out of a
% 60000-element array and every other reference dropped, the next
% 60000-element array costs the full 960000 bytes rather than nothing. A
% 42-byte window out of a 60000-byte string likewise leaves the next such
% string costing full price. Dropping the window as well as the host reclaims
% everything, so the window is what holds it.
% An Adobe interpreter reclaims all but the window: "the only live reference
% is to a 42-element subinterval near the middle of the original large array;
% an Adobe PostScript interpreter ... will in fact reclaim all of the array
% except those 42 elements, on the next collection."
% The PLRM does not require this, so the retention is not a conformance
% failure; it is recorded because a program that slices a small window out of
% a large buffer and keeps the window keeps the buffer, which in a context
% that serves many jobs reads as a leak. Asserting the present behaviour
% would freeze it, so it is written down for the collector work instead.

failcount 0 eq { (SUCCESS) = } { (FAILURES: ) print failcount = } ifelse
